# Repo-wide line-ending pin.
#
# agent-onboarding/.gitattributes carries the same two rules, but it only reaches
# agent-onboarding/**. In this repository the plugin is published at management-portal/**
# — the directory is renamed on the way over from the source repo — so every hook script
# the plugin actually runs sits OUTSIDE that file's reach, and has since the rename.
#
# The blobs are LF today only because the machine that publishes has core.autocrlf=true.
# That is a local git config, not a property of the repository: a clone with a different
# setting can commit CRLF into scripts/*.js and nothing here would object. That breaks the
# `#!/usr/bin/env node` shebang for anyone running a script directly — which 1.5.0 makes a
# documented path, since standing a gate down by hand is
# `node "<plugin>/scripts/canon-gate.js" stand-down --gate <ID>`.
#
# Pinning at the root covers both trees and keeps the two repos byte-identical, which is
# what makes a blob-SHA comparison a usable way to verify a publish.
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
